1
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:10,420
And now the goddess put it in her mind — gray-eyed Athena,

2
00:00:10,420 --> 00:00:14,220
in the mind of Icarius' daughter,
watchful-minded Penelope —

3
00:00:14,220 --> 00:00:16,780
to set before the suitors the bow and the gray iron

4
00:00:16,780 --> 00:00:21,480
in the halls of Odysseus: the contest,
and the beginning of the slaughter.

5
00:00:21,480 --> 00:00:26,020
She climbed the high stairway of her house
and took the bent key in her strong hand —

6
00:00:26,020 --> 00:00:31,100
a beautiful key of bronze, with an ivory handle —
and went her way with her attendant women

7
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:34,980
to the last storeroom. There the master's treasures lay:

8
00:00:34,980 --> 00:00:38,160
bronze, and gold, and iron wrought with labor.

9
00:00:38,160 --> 00:00:44,060
And there lay the backsprung bow, and the quiver for
the arrows, with many groan-laden shafts inside —

10
00:00:44,060 --> 00:00:47,380
gifts that a guest-friend gave
him when they met in Lacedaemon:

11
00:00:47,380 --> 00:00:50,960
Iphitus, Eurytus' son, a man like the immortals.

12
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:55,760
The two had crossed paths in Messene,
in the house of thoughtful Ortilochus. Odysseus

13
00:00:55,760 --> 00:00:58,500
had come to collect a debt the whole people owed him:

14
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:01,340
for Messenian men had lifted sheep out of Ithaca —

15
00:01:01,340 --> 00:01:04,860
three hundred, with their herdsmen —
in their many-benched ships.

16
00:01:04,860 --> 00:01:08,100
For their sake Odysseus came that long road on embassy,

17
00:01:08,100 --> 00:01:11,600
a boy still, sent out by his father and the other elders.

18
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:17,780
And Iphitus came hunting horses — twelve mares he had lost,
with hard-working mules at suck beneath them —

19
00:01:17,780 --> 00:01:22,840
the same mares that afterward became his death and doom,
when he came to the strong-hearted son of Zeus,

20
00:01:22,840 --> 00:01:28,620
the man Heracles, that expert in great deeds,
who killed him — a guest in his own house.

21
00:01:28,620 --> 00:01:31,940
Ruthless: no shame before the gods'
watching eye, nor for the table

22
00:01:31,940 --> 00:01:37,920
he himself had set the man; he killed him even so,
and kept the strong-hoofed mares in his own halls.

23
00:01:37,920 --> 00:01:41,540
Asking after these, Iphitus met
Odysseus, and gave him the bow

24
00:01:41,540 --> 00:01:46,780
that great Eurytus carried in the old days,
and left to his son at his death in the high house.

25
00:01:46,780 --> 00:01:50,260
And Odysseus gave him a sharp sword and a strong spear —

26
00:01:50,260 --> 00:01:52,640
the start of a binding friendship; but they never knew

27
00:01:52,640 --> 00:01:59,440
each other's tables: before that, the son of Zeus killed
Iphitus, Eurytus' son, that man like the immortals,

28
00:01:59,440 --> 00:02:04,660
who gave the bow. And noble Odysseus never took
it with him going to war in the black ships:

29
00:02:04,660 --> 00:02:10,280
it stood in his halls at home, a memorial of a
beloved guest-friend; on his own land he carried it.

30
00:02:10,280 --> 00:02:13,480
And when she reached that storeroom,
the shining among women,

31
00:02:13,480 --> 00:02:18,860
and stepped up on the oak threshold — which a
carpenter once planed with skill and trued to the line,

32
00:02:18,860 --> 00:02:22,180
and fixed the doorposts in it,
and hung the shining doors —

33
00:02:22,180 --> 00:02:27,140
at once she quickly loosed the thong from the hook,
slid in the key, and shot back the bolts of the doors

34
00:02:27,140 --> 00:02:32,900
with a straight thrust. And they bellowed like a bull
at pasture in a meadow — so loud the beautiful doors

35
00:02:32,900 --> 00:02:36,140
bellowed at the key's stroke;
and they flew open before her.

36
00:02:36,140 --> 00:02:41,620
She stepped up onto the high floor, where the chests
stood full of clothing laid away in fragrance.

37
00:02:41,620 --> 00:02:45,980
Reaching up from there she took the bow off its peg,
bright in the bow-case that enclosed it.

38
00:02:45,980 --> 00:02:47,580
With the case on her knees,

39
00:02:47,580 --> 00:02:51,500
and wept aloud, and shrill, and drew out the master's bow.

40
00:02:51,500 --> 00:02:56,860
And when she had taken her fill of tear-heavy mourning,
she went her way to the hall, to the lordly suitors,

41
00:02:56,860 --> 00:03:03,180
holding in her hand the backsprung bow and the quiver
for the arrows, with many groan-laden shafts inside;

42
00:03:03,180 --> 00:03:05,580
and beside her handmaids carried the box where the iron

43
00:03:05,580 --> 00:03:09,480
lay in plenty, and the bronze:
the master's gear of contest.

44
00:03:09,480 --> 00:03:15,220
And when she reached the suitors, shining among women,
she stood beside the pillar of the close-built roof,

45
00:03:15,220 --> 00:03:20,000
holding her glistening veil before her cheeks,
a trusted handmaid standing on either side,

46
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:23,300
and spoke at once among the suitors, and said her word:

47
00:03:23,300 --> 00:03:26,220
"Hear me, you lordly suitors,
who have fastened on this house

48
00:03:26,220 --> 00:03:29,420
to eat and drink your way through it without ever a pause,

49
00:03:29,420 --> 00:03:36,100
its master long gone; nor could you offer any
other excuse in all this time, any word at all,

50
00:03:36,100 --> 00:03:38,940
except your lust to marry me and take me to wife.

51
00:03:38,940 --> 00:03:43,680
Well then, you suitors — come:
here stands your prize before you.

52
00:03:43,680 --> 00:03:46,660
I set out the great bow of godlike Odysseus.

53
00:03:46,660 --> 00:03:51,740
Whoever strings the bow most easily in his hands
and shoots an arrow through all twelve axes —

54
00:03:51,740 --> 00:03:58,000
him I will follow, forsaking this house, this house
of my marriage, this beautiful house, full of all

55
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:02,620
that sustains life. I think I shall
remember it even in my dreams."

56
00:04:02,620 --> 00:04:09,300
So she spoke, and told Eumaeus, the noble swineherd,
to set the bow before the suitors, and the gray iron.

57
00:04:09,300 --> 00:04:12,340
In tears Eumaeus took them and laid them down;

58
00:04:12,340 --> 00:04:16,560
and the cowherd wept on his side,
when he saw the master's bow.

59
00:04:16,560 --> 00:04:20,060
And Antinous rebuked them,
and spoke a word, and named them:

60
00:04:20,060 --> 00:04:23,960
"Idiot rustics! — brains that stop at the day's end!

61
00:04:23,960 --> 00:04:26,780
Wretched pair, why are you
streaming tears, and stirring up

62
00:04:26,780 --> 00:04:29,900
the heart in the woman's breast?
Her heart lies deep enough

63
00:04:29,900 --> 00:04:33,480
in grief already, since she lost her dear husband.

64
00:04:33,480 --> 00:04:39,460
Sit and feast in silence — or take your bawling outside,
the two of you, and leave the bow right here,

65
00:04:39,460 --> 00:04:42,920
a trial for the suitors none will
slip through: for I do not think

66
00:04:42,920 --> 00:04:45,760
this polished bow is going to be strung with ease.

67
00:04:45,760 --> 00:04:50,840
There is no man in this whole company
such as Odysseus was. I saw him myself —

68
00:04:50,840 --> 00:04:54,100
I remember it well — though I was still a witless child."

69
00:04:54,100 --> 00:04:59,740
So he spoke; but the heart in his breast was hoping
that he would string the bow and shoot through the iron.

70
00:04:59,740 --> 00:05:02,040
In fact he was to be the first to taste an arrow

71
00:05:02,040 --> 00:05:05,820
from the hands of blameless Odysseus —
the man he was dishonoring

72
00:05:05,820 --> 00:05:10,060
that moment, sitting in his hall,
and egging on all his companions.

73
00:05:10,060 --> 00:05:13,600
And the sacred strength of Telemachus spoke among them:

74
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:16,760
"Ah — Zeus the son of Cronos has made me witless!

75
00:05:16,760 --> 00:05:22,120
My own dear mother — sensible as she is — declares
she will follow another man and forsake this house;

76
00:05:22,120 --> 00:05:24,800
and I laugh, and enjoy it, with my witless heart.

77
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:28,660
Come then, you suitors — since here stands your prize:

78
00:05:28,660 --> 00:05:34,540
a woman whose like there is not now in all Achaea,
not in holy Pylos, not in Argos or Mycenae,

79
00:05:34,540 --> 00:05:37,140
not on Ithaca itself, not on the dark mainland —

80
00:05:37,140 --> 00:05:40,700
but you know that yourselves.
Why should I praise my mother?

81
00:05:40,700 --> 00:05:46,340
Come — no dodges, no dragging it out; no more hanging
back from the stringing of the bow. Let us see it!

82
00:05:46,340 --> 00:05:49,160
And I myself might make a trial of the bow as well:

83
00:05:49,160 --> 00:05:53,560
for if I string it, and shoot through the iron,
then it would be no grief to me that my honored mother

84
00:05:53,560 --> 00:05:57,420
should leave this house and follow
another man — since I would stay behind

85
00:05:57,420 --> 00:06:00,860
man enough at last to lift my father's beautiful prizes."

86
00:06:00,860 --> 00:06:03,960
He spoke, and flung the crimson cloak from his shoulders,

87
00:06:03,960 --> 00:06:07,920
rising to his full height, and put
off the sharp sword from his shoulder.

88
00:06:07,920 --> 00:06:12,980
First he set up the axes, digging one long
trench for all of them, trued to a line,

89
00:06:12,980 --> 00:06:16,080
and stamped the earth firm round them.
Amazement took every man

90
00:06:16,080 --> 00:06:20,660
who watched — how squarely he set them,
who had never seen it before.

91
00:06:20,660 --> 00:06:24,720
Then he went and stood on the
threshold and began to try the bow.

92
00:06:24,720 --> 00:06:28,120
Three times he set it shaking, hot to draw it home;

93
00:06:28,120 --> 00:06:33,940
three times he eased his force, hoping still in his
heart to string the cord and shoot through the iron.

94
00:06:33,940 --> 00:06:37,380
And now, hauling a fourth time,
his strength would have strung it —

95
00:06:37,380 --> 00:06:41,220
but Odysseus shook his head,
and checked him, keen as he was.

96
00:06:41,220 --> 00:06:44,260
And the sacred strength of
Telemachus spoke among them again:

97
00:06:44,260 --> 00:06:48,640
"Ah — so I will be a weakling and a coward all my days!

98
00:06:48,640 --> 00:06:54,280
Or I am too young yet, and cannot trust my hands
to defend a man when someone picks a quarrel first.

99
00:06:54,280 --> 00:07:00,200
Come then, you who are stronger than I am —
come try the bow, and let us finish out the contest."

100
00:07:00,200 --> 00:07:06,280
So speaking he set the bow away from him on the ground,
leaning it against the joined and polished panels,

101
00:07:06,280 --> 00:07:09,460
with the swift arrow propped on the fine door-hook there,

102
00:07:09,460 --> 00:07:12,600
and sat back down on the chair from which he had risen.

103
00:07:12,600 --> 00:07:15,700
And Antinous spoke among them, Eupeithes' son:

104
00:07:15,700 --> 00:07:20,960
"Up, all you companions — in order, left to right,
starting from where the wine is always poured."

105
00:07:20,960 --> 00:07:24,580
So spoke Antinous, and his word pleased them well.

106
00:07:24,580 --> 00:07:30,440
And the first to rise was Leodes, the son of Oenops,
who was their sacrifice-diviner, and always sat

107
00:07:30,440 --> 00:07:33,980
in the farthest corner by the
beautiful mixing-bowl: to him alone

108
00:07:33,980 --> 00:07:38,560
their reckless outrages were hateful;
he was indignant at all the suitors.

109
00:07:38,560 --> 00:07:41,580
He now was first to take the bow and the swift arrow,

110
00:07:41,580 --> 00:07:45,140
and went and stood on the
threshold and began to try the bow.

111
00:07:45,140 --> 00:07:48,200
He could not string it:
long before that, hauling, his hands

112
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:52,560
gave out — his soft, unhardened hands;
and he said among the suitors:

113
00:07:52,560 --> 00:07:55,600
"Friends, I cannot bend it. Let another take it now.

114
00:07:55,600 --> 00:08:00,320
Many chief men this bow is going to strip of
life and breath — since truly it is better far

115
00:08:00,320 --> 00:08:06,500
to die than live on and miss the mark we gather
here for day upon day, forever, waiting on hope.

116
00:08:06,500 --> 00:08:11,560
Now every man of you hopes, every heart is
set on marrying Penelope, Odysseus' bedmate.

117
00:08:11,560 --> 00:08:16,620
But once he has tried the bow, and seen — well,
let him woo some other fair-robed Achaean woman

118
00:08:16,620 --> 00:08:20,160
with his bride-gifts then, and seek her; and let this one

119
00:08:20,160 --> 00:08:23,380
marry the man who gives the most, and comes as her fate."

120
00:08:23,380 --> 00:08:29,260
So he spoke his say, and set the bow away from him,
leaning it against the joined and polished panels,

121
00:08:29,260 --> 00:08:32,140
with the swift arrow propped on the fine door-hook there,

122
00:08:32,140 --> 00:08:34,920
and sat back down on the chair from which he had risen.

123
00:08:34,920 --> 00:08:38,460
And Antinous rebuked him, and spoke a word, and named him:

124
00:08:38,460 --> 00:08:41,300
"Leodes — what a word has slipped the fence of your teeth!

125
00:08:41,300 --> 00:08:44,980
A dreadful word, a bitter one —
I am angry just to hear it —

126
00:08:44,980 --> 00:08:49,980
that this bow, if you please, will strip our chief
men of life and breath — because you cannot string it!

127
00:08:49,980 --> 00:08:55,120
No: your honored mother did not bear you the kind
of man who has it in him to draw a bow and shoot;

128
00:08:55,120 --> 00:08:58,000
but the other lordly suitors will string it, and soon."

129
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:02,000
So he spoke, and gave his orders
to Melanthius the goatherd:

130
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:07,500
"Look alive, Melanthius — light a fire in the hall,
and set a big bench by it with a fleece on top,

131
00:09:07,500 --> 00:09:10,560
and bring out the great wheel
of lard from the stores inside,

132
00:09:10,560 --> 00:09:16,120
so we young men can warm the bow, and grease it with
the fat, and try it, and finish out the contest."

133
00:09:16,120 --> 00:09:20,000
So he spoke; and Melanthius quickly
kindled the unwearying fire,

134
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:22,760
and carried up the bench and set
it there with a fleece on it,

135
00:09:22,760 --> 00:09:26,060
and brought out the great wheel
of lard from the stores inside.

136
00:09:26,060 --> 00:09:29,620
With that the young men warmed the
bow and made their trials — and still

137
00:09:29,620 --> 00:09:33,120
they could not string it: they fell
far short of the strength it took.

138
00:09:33,120 --> 00:09:36,820
But Antinous still held off, and godlike Eurymachus,

139
00:09:36,820 --> 00:09:40,660
the suitors' chiefs: in prowess
they were far the best of them.

140
00:09:40,660 --> 00:09:45,920
Now out of the house those two had gone together,
the cowherd and the swineherd of godlike Odysseus,

141
00:09:45,920 --> 00:09:49,940
and after them Odysseus himself, the noble one, came out.

142
00:09:49,940 --> 00:09:52,640
And when they were outside the doors and the courtyard,

143
00:09:52,640 --> 00:09:56,140
he raised his voice and spoke to them with gentle words:

144
00:09:56,140 --> 00:09:59,360
"Cowherd — and you, swineherd —
shall I say a thing to you,

145
00:09:59,360 --> 00:10:02,620
or keep it to myself? My heart tells me to say it.

146
00:10:02,620 --> 00:10:05,280
What kind of men would you be, to fight for Odysseus,

147
00:10:05,280 --> 00:10:10,120
if he came from somewhere — like that,
out of nowhere — some god bringing him?

148
00:10:10,120 --> 00:10:12,580
Would you fight for the suitors, or for Odysseus?

149
00:10:12,580 --> 00:10:15,640
Speak out, as your heart and spirit bid you."

150
00:10:15,640 --> 00:10:19,020
Then the cowherd answered him, the master of the oxen:

151
00:10:19,020 --> 00:10:22,160
"Father Zeus, if only you would accomplish this desire:

152
00:10:22,160 --> 00:10:25,900
that the man himself come back, and a god guide him home —

153
00:10:25,900 --> 00:10:29,500
then you would learn my strength,
and how my hands follow it!"

154
00:10:29,500 --> 00:10:32,000
And in the same way Eumaeus prayed to all the gods

155
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:36,140
that Odysseus of the many designs
might come home to his own house.

156
00:10:36,140 --> 00:10:41,440
And when he had proof of the true mind in both of them,
he found words again, and said to them in answer:

157
00:10:41,440 --> 00:10:46,200
"He is home — here — I am the man myself.
After suffering many pains,

158
00:10:46,200 --> 00:10:49,060
I have come in the twentieth year to my fathers' land.

159
00:10:49,060 --> 00:10:55,400
And I know that of all my slaves, to you two only my
coming is what you prayed for. Of the rest I have heard

160
00:10:55,400 --> 00:10:58,100
not one man praying that I come home again.

161
00:10:58,100 --> 00:11:01,280
So to you two I will tell the
whole truth of what shall be.

162
00:11:01,280 --> 00:11:04,280
If a god brings the haughty suitors down beneath my hands,

163
00:11:04,280 --> 00:11:10,600
I will find wives for you both, and give you possessions,
and houses built near mine; and from that day

164
00:11:10,600 --> 00:11:13,880
you shall be to me the companions
and brothers of Telemachus.

165
00:11:13,880 --> 00:11:20,080
And come — I will show you a plain sign besides,
so you may know me for certain and trust it in your hearts:

166
00:11:20,080 --> 00:11:23,020
the scar — the wound the boar dealt me with its white tusk

167
00:11:23,020 --> 00:11:26,960
when I went to Parnassus with the sons of Autolycus."

168
00:11:26,960 --> 00:11:31,000
So speaking, he drew the rags back from the great scar.

169
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:34,220
And when the two had seen it, and taken in each detail,

170
00:11:34,220 --> 00:11:38,040
they wept, and threw their arms round thoughtful Odysseus,

171
00:11:38,040 --> 00:11:41,140
and kissed his head and shoulders, welcoming him home;

172
00:11:41,140 --> 00:11:44,780
and in the same way Odysseus kissed their heads and hands.

173
00:11:44,780 --> 00:11:47,780
And the light of the sun would
have gone down on their weeping

174
00:11:47,780 --> 00:11:50,460
had not Odysseus himself checked them, and said:

175
00:11:50,460 --> 00:11:56,460
"Enough of weeping and wailing — someone coming out
of the hall might see, and tell them there inside.

176
00:11:56,460 --> 00:11:59,640
Go back in one at a time, not all together:

177
00:11:59,640 --> 00:12:03,400
I first, you after. And let this be the signal:

178
00:12:03,400 --> 00:12:08,300
all the others — all the lordly suitors —
will refuse to let the bow and quiver be given to me;

179
00:12:08,300 --> 00:12:13,600
but you, noble Eumaeus, carry the bow up the
hall and set it in my hands; and tell the women

180
00:12:13,600 --> 00:12:18,440
to bar the close-fitted doors of their quarters,
and if any one of them hears groaning or the crash of men

181
00:12:18,440 --> 00:12:24,160
inside our walls, let her not run out,
but stay where she is, at her work, in silence.

182
00:12:24,160 --> 00:12:28,020
And you, noble Philoetius:
your charge is the courtyard gates —

183
00:12:28,020 --> 00:12:31,660
bolt them with the bar,
and quickly lash the fastening on."

184
00:12:31,660 --> 00:12:36,320
So speaking he entered the well-settled house and
went and sat on the stool from which he had risen;

185
00:12:36,320 --> 00:12:41,040
and then the two slaves of godlike Odysseus came in too.

186
00:12:41,040 --> 00:12:46,400
Eurymachus by now was handling the bow, warming
it this side and that at the glow of the fire —

187
00:12:46,400 --> 00:12:50,620
and even so he could not string it.
His proud heart groaned aloud,

188
00:12:50,620 --> 00:12:53,900
and bursting out in anger he spoke a word and named it:

189
00:12:53,900 --> 00:12:58,120
"Damn it — here is a grief for me,
for myself and for us all!

190
00:12:58,120 --> 00:13:01,560
It is not the marriage I mourn
so much, though that stings too:

191
00:13:01,560 --> 00:13:07,940
there are plenty of other Achaean women, some right
here in sea-circled Ithaca, some in other towns.

192
00:13:07,940 --> 00:13:12,480
No — it is that we fall so far short in strength
of godlike Odysseus that we cannot string

193
00:13:12,480 --> 00:13:16,600
his bow. A disgrace for even men unborn to hear of!"

194
00:13:16,600 --> 00:13:19,780
Then Antinous answered him, Eupeithes' son:

195
00:13:19,780 --> 00:13:23,200
"Eurymachus, it will not stand so —
and you know it yourself.

196
00:13:23,200 --> 00:13:29,220
Today, through all the land, is the feast of the god —
his holy day. Who could be bending bows? No —

197
00:13:29,220 --> 00:13:32,360
set it down and be easy. And the axes: why not leave them

198
00:13:32,360 --> 00:13:36,140
standing, all of them? No one,
I think, will carry them off —

199
00:13:36,140 --> 00:13:39,680
no thief is coming into the hall of Laertes' son Odysseus.

200
00:13:39,680 --> 00:13:42,600
Come — let the cupbearer pour first-drops in the cups,

201
00:13:42,600 --> 00:13:48,000
so we can make libation, and lay aside the curved bow;
and in the morning tell Melanthius the goatherd

202
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:50,700
to drive in goats, the pick of all his herds,

203
00:13:50,700 --> 00:13:54,660
so we can lay thigh-pieces on the
altar of Apollo, lord of the bow,

204
00:13:54,660 --> 00:13:58,040
and then try the bow, and finish out the contest."

205
00:13:58,040 --> 00:14:01,120
So spoke Antinous, and his word pleased them well.

206
00:14:01,120 --> 00:14:05,760
And heralds poured water over their hands,
and young men crowned the mixing-bowls with drink

207
00:14:05,760 --> 00:14:09,500
and served it round to all, with first-drops in the cups.

208
00:14:09,500 --> 00:14:12,700
And when they had poured, and drunk
as much as their hearts would have,

209
00:14:12,700 --> 00:14:17,160
Odysseus of the many designs,
with craft in mind, spoke among them:

210
00:14:17,160 --> 00:14:23,480
"Hear me, you suitors of the far-famed queen,
while I say what the heart in my breast commands.

211
00:14:23,480 --> 00:14:26,900
To Eurymachus above all, and to godlike Antinous,

212
00:14:26,900 --> 00:14:30,840
I make my plea — since he spoke
this word too as it should be:

213
00:14:30,840 --> 00:14:34,160
for now, let the bow rest; turn it over to the gods;

214
00:14:34,160 --> 00:14:37,680
and in the morning the god will
give the mastery to whom he will.

215
00:14:37,680 --> 00:14:43,320
But come — give me the polished bow, so among you I
can try my hands and my strength: whether the force

216
00:14:43,320 --> 00:14:48,920
is still there, that once lived in my supple limbs,
or wandering and neglect have destroyed it by now."

217
00:14:48,920 --> 00:14:54,880
So he spoke; and they were all outraged past measure,
in dread that he would string the polished bow.

218
00:14:54,880 --> 00:14:58,440
And Antinous rebuked him, and spoke a word, and named him:

219
00:14:58,440 --> 00:15:02,940
"You wretched stranger —
not a grain of sense in you, not a trace!

220
00:15:02,940 --> 00:15:06,200
Is it not enough that you feast at your ease with us,

221
00:15:06,200 --> 00:15:09,900
the great ones — never stinted of the meal, listening in

222
00:15:09,900 --> 00:15:16,680
on all our speeches and our talk? No other stranger,
no beggar, gets to listen to our speeches.

223
00:15:16,680 --> 00:15:20,180
It is the wine that has you — the honey-sweet: it damages

224
00:15:20,180 --> 00:15:24,060
any man who gulps it wide-mouthed past his measure.

225
00:15:24,060 --> 00:15:30,440
Wine — it was wine that maddened the Centaur,
famous Eurytion, in the hall of great-hearted Peirithous,

226
00:15:30,440 --> 00:15:34,980
on his visit to the Lapiths.
And when the wine had maddened his wits,

227
00:15:34,980 --> 00:15:38,020
raving mad he did his evil in Peirithous' house;

228
00:15:38,020 --> 00:15:41,360
and grief seized the heroes,
and they leapt up and dragged him

229
00:15:41,360 --> 00:15:44,140
out through the porch to the open air, and shore away

230
00:15:44,140 --> 00:15:48,200
his ears and nose with the
pitiless bronze; and he, his wits

231
00:15:48,200 --> 00:15:52,240
destroyed, went off in his witless
heart, hauling his ruin with him.

232
00:15:52,240 --> 00:15:59,060
From that came the feud between Centaurs and men;
but he found the evil first for himself, heavy with wine.

233
00:15:59,060 --> 00:16:04,380
Just so I proclaim great trouble for you,
if you should string that bow. You will meet no kindness

234
00:16:04,380 --> 00:16:07,860
in this land of ours — we will pack
you straight off in a black ship

235
00:16:07,860 --> 00:16:14,540
to King Echetus, the maimer of all mankind,
and out of his hands nothing will save you. Sit easy,

236
00:16:14,540 --> 00:16:18,160
drink, and do not set yourself against younger men."

237
00:16:18,160 --> 00:16:20,960
Then watchful-minded Penelope answered him:

238
00:16:20,960 --> 00:16:24,160
"Antinous — it is not fine, and it is not just, to stint

239
00:16:24,160 --> 00:16:27,440
the guests of Telemachus, whoever comes to this house.

240
00:16:27,440 --> 00:16:33,040
Do you think — if this stranger strings the great bow
of Odysseus, trusting his hands and his own strength —

241
00:16:33,040 --> 00:16:35,660
that he will lead me home and make me his wife?

242
00:16:35,660 --> 00:16:38,280
He himself has no such hope in his breast.

243
00:16:38,280 --> 00:16:40,240
Let none of you sit at the feast here grieving

244
00:16:40,240 --> 00:16:44,840
over that: it would not be seemly —
no, and it is not thinkable."

245
00:16:44,840 --> 00:16:47,480
Then Eurymachus, Polybus' son, answered her:

246
00:16:47,480 --> 00:16:53,680
"Daughter of Icarius, watchful-minded Penelope,
it is not that we think he will lead you off — unthinkable —

247
00:16:53,680 --> 00:16:56,000
but we shrink from the talk of men and women:

248
00:16:56,000 --> 00:17:01,360
that one of the baser Achaeans may say someday,
'Men far weaker are wooing a blameless man's

249
00:17:01,360 --> 00:17:07,240
wife — and they cannot even string his polished bow;
and then some other man, a wandering beggar, came

250
00:17:07,240 --> 00:17:10,240
and strung the bow with ease, and shot through the iron.'

251
00:17:10,240 --> 00:17:14,180
So they will say; and for us that
would be shame heaped on shame."

252
00:17:14,180 --> 00:17:16,740
Then watchful-minded Penelope answered him:

253
00:17:16,740 --> 00:17:22,040
"Eurymachus — there is no fair fame in the land,
and cannot be, for men who dishonor and devour the house

254
00:17:22,040 --> 00:17:25,100
of their better. Why count this thing your shame?

255
00:17:25,100 --> 00:17:31,300
The stranger here is very tall, and firmly built,
and declares himself the son of a well-born father.

256
00:17:31,300 --> 00:17:34,140
Come — give him the polished bow. Let us see it.

257
00:17:34,140 --> 00:17:36,800
For I will say this outright, and it will be accomplished:

258
00:17:36,800 --> 00:17:42,940
if he strings it — if Apollo grants him that glory —
I will dress him in a cloak and a tunic, fine clothing,

259
00:17:42,940 --> 00:17:45,960
and give him a sharp javelin, to keep off dogs and men,

260
00:17:45,960 --> 00:17:49,620
and a two-edged sword; and I will
give him sandals for his feet,

261
00:17:49,620 --> 00:17:52,340
and send him wherever his heart and spirit bid him go."

262
00:17:52,340 --> 00:17:55,240
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered her:

263
00:17:55,240 --> 00:18:01,080
"Mother mine — the bow: no Achaean has better right
than I to give it or to refuse it to whomever I will —

264
00:18:01,080 --> 00:18:06,980
not one of all who lord it through rocky Ithaca,
nor in the islands over toward horse-grazing Elis:

265
00:18:06,980 --> 00:18:10,560
not one of them shall force me
against my will, not even if I choose

266
00:18:10,560 --> 00:18:13,940
to give the stranger this bow
outright, to carry off for good.

267
00:18:13,940 --> 00:18:19,040
Go into the house and see to your own work,
the loom and the distaff, and command your handmaids

268
00:18:19,040 --> 00:18:22,220
to go about their work. The bow belongs to men —

269
00:18:22,220 --> 00:18:26,560
to all men, and to me above all:
mine is the power in this house."

270
00:18:26,560 --> 00:18:31,120
In wonder, she went back into the house,
for she laid her son's clear-headed word to heart.

271
00:18:31,120 --> 00:18:37,180
Climbing to her upper room with her serving-women,
she wept there for Odysseus, her dear husband, until

272
00:18:37,180 --> 00:18:40,540
gray-eyed Athena cast sweet sleep upon her eyelids.

273
00:18:40,540 --> 00:18:45,040
And the noble swineherd had taken up
the curved bow and was carrying it —

274
00:18:45,040 --> 00:18:48,940
and the suitors, all of them,
burst out shouting through the halls;

275
00:18:48,940 --> 00:18:51,700
and a man of the arrogant young men would say:

276
00:18:51,700 --> 00:18:55,540
"Where are you carrying that curved bow,
you pitiful swineherd —

277
00:18:55,540 --> 00:19:00,340
you drifter? Soon enough,
out with your pigs, alone, away from men,

278
00:19:00,340 --> 00:19:04,000
the swift dogs you raised
yourself will eat you — if Apollo

279
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:08,100
will only be gracious to us,
and the other deathless gods."

280
00:19:08,100 --> 00:19:13,160
So they spoke; and he set the bow down
where he stood, carrying it no further,

281
00:19:13,160 --> 00:19:16,920
frightened, because the crowd of
them was shouting through the halls.

282
00:19:16,920 --> 00:19:20,440
But Telemachus, from the other side,
called out his threat:

283
00:19:20,440 --> 00:19:25,640
"Old father — carry the bow on.
You will soon be sorry, obeying them all.

284
00:19:25,640 --> 00:19:29,300
Watch out, or younger though I am
I will chase you into the fields

285
00:19:29,300 --> 00:19:33,320
pelting you with stones. In strength I am the better man.

286
00:19:33,320 --> 00:19:38,220
If only I stood as far above all the suitors in
this house — in force of hands and strength —

287
00:19:38,220 --> 00:19:44,140
I would soon send more than one of them off in misery,
out of our house: for they devise evils."

288
00:19:44,140 --> 00:19:50,320
So he spoke; and all the suitors laughed at him sweetly,
and let go their bitter anger against Telemachus.

289
00:19:50,320 --> 00:19:56,740
And the swineherd carried the bow up the hall and
stood by thoughtful Odysseus and set it in his hands.

290
00:19:56,740 --> 00:19:59,540
Then he called out the nurse Eurycleia and said:

291
00:19:59,540 --> 00:20:02,980
"Telemachus commands you, watchful-minded Eurycleia:

292
00:20:02,980 --> 00:20:07,900
bar the close-fitted doors of the women's hall,
and if any woman hears groaning or the crash of men

293
00:20:07,900 --> 00:20:13,720
inside our walls, let her not run out,
but stay where she is, at her work, in silence."

294
00:20:13,720 --> 00:20:16,840
So he spoke; and for her the word stayed wingless.

295
00:20:16,840 --> 00:20:19,820
And she barred the doors of the well-settled halls.

296
00:20:19,820 --> 00:20:25,260
In silence Philoetius slipped out of the house
and barred the gates of the well-fenced courtyard.

297
00:20:25,260 --> 00:20:27,560
Under the colonnade there lay a curved ship's cable

298
00:20:27,560 --> 00:20:32,040
of byblus fiber; with it he lashed
the gates, and went back in,

299
00:20:32,040 --> 00:20:34,640
and went and sat on the stool from which he had risen,

300
00:20:34,640 --> 00:20:38,760
with his eyes on Odysseus.
And he was handling the bow already,

301
00:20:38,760 --> 00:20:41,640
turning it round and round, testing it this way and that,

302
00:20:41,640 --> 00:20:45,000
for worms that might have eaten
the horn while its master was away.

303
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:47,560
And a man would say, with a glance at his neighbor:

304
00:20:47,560 --> 00:20:51,780
"Quite the connoisseur of bows! An expert eye — a thief's!

305
00:20:51,780 --> 00:20:57,560
No doubt he has bows like this one laid up at home —
or means to make one. Look how he turns it over,

306
00:20:57,560 --> 00:21:00,860
this way and that — the vagrant schooled in trouble!"

307
00:21:00,860 --> 00:21:02,980
And another of the arrogant young men would say:

308
00:21:02,980 --> 00:21:08,660
"May all his profit in life come out exactly as
his luck in ever managing to string that thing!"

309
00:21:08,660 --> 00:21:12,480
So the suitors talked. But Odysseus of the many designs,

310
00:21:12,480 --> 00:21:15,380
the moment he had hefted the
great bow and eyed it everywhere —

311
00:21:15,380 --> 00:21:20,880
as when a man skilled in the lyre and in singing
easily stretches a string around a new peg,

312
00:21:20,880 --> 00:21:23,700
making the twisted sheep-gut fast at either end —

313
00:21:23,700 --> 00:21:28,020
so without haste, without effort,
Odysseus strung the great bow.

314
00:21:28,020 --> 00:21:31,180
Then his right hand took hold and tried the string,

315
00:21:31,180 --> 00:21:35,020
and under his touch it sang sweet, like a swallow's voice.

316
00:21:35,020 --> 00:21:41,780
A great ache took the suitors; the color changed in
every face; and Zeus thundered loud, showing his signs.

317
00:21:41,780 --> 00:21:47,780
And much-enduring noble Odysseus was glad that the
son of crooked-counseled Cronos sent him an omen;

318
00:21:47,780 --> 00:21:50,460
and he took up the swift arrow that lay bare on the table

319
00:21:50,460 --> 00:21:54,140
beside him — the rest were
stored inside the hollow quiver,

320
00:21:54,140 --> 00:21:56,860
the ones the Achaeans were shortly going to taste.

321
00:21:56,860 --> 00:22:00,580
He set it on the bridge of the bow,
drew back the string and the notched butt

322
00:22:00,580 --> 00:22:06,880
from the very stool where he sat, and let the arrow fly
with a straight aim, and did not miss a single axe —

323
00:22:06,880 --> 00:22:09,900
from the first helve-ring clean on through: the arrow

324
00:22:09,900 --> 00:22:14,440
heavy with bronze passed through and out.
And he said to Telemachus:

325
00:22:14,440 --> 00:22:17,180
"Telemachus — the guest who sits in your halls does not

326
00:22:17,180 --> 00:22:20,440
disgrace you: I did not miss the mark, and I was not long

327
00:22:20,440 --> 00:22:24,120
about stringing the bow. My strength stands yet unbroken —

328
00:22:24,120 --> 00:22:27,500
not as the suitors' taunts would
have it, in their disrespect.

329
00:22:27,500 --> 00:22:31,300
But now it is time that supper
too be made for the Achaeans,

330
00:22:31,300 --> 00:22:34,760
while there is light — and after that,
the other entertainments:

331
00:22:34,760 --> 00:22:38,120
the dance and the lyre.
For these are the garnish of a feast."

332
00:22:38,120 --> 00:22:43,340
He spoke, and signaled with his brows.
And he slung on his sharp sword,

333
00:22:43,340 --> 00:22:49,620
Telemachus, the dear son of godlike Odysseus,
and closed his hand around his spear, and there beside him,

334
00:22:49,620 --> 00:22:53,568
close by his chair, he stood —
armed in the flashing bronze.
